WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.234 --> 00:00:02.530
- [Narrator] When it comes
to colonizing new worlds,

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:04.890
Mars can be humanity's best option.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.170
But the journey won't be
like it was in the past

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:10.966
for pioneers like Magellan,
Gagarin, and Neil Armstrong.

00:00:10.966 --> 00:00:14.645
This new frontier would be the
most extreme challenge yet.

00:00:14.645 --> 00:00:17.890
It's not just learning how
to survive on an alien planet

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:20.880
with less oxygen, a
weaker gravitational pull,

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.404
and more harmful radiation.

00:00:22.404 --> 00:00:26.030
It's enduring the changes
that these extreme conditions

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:29.081
will have on the human body,
manipulating it in ways

00:00:29.081 --> 00:00:31.853
that we can only begin to imagine.

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:35.380
Even astronauts know that
you don't have to spend

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:38.220
much time off earth to notice changes.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.040
The lower gravity can kickstart

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:42.299
a whole list of physical alterations.

00:00:42.299 --> 00:00:44.170
Just ask this guy.

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:46.820
Former NASA astronaut, Scott Kelly.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:48.600
He lived for an entire year

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.530
on the International Space Station.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:52.300
Without Earth's gravitational pull,

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:55.150
the zero gravity weakened
his bones and muscles

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:57.460
and expanded the space
between his vertebrae,

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:59.614
making him two inches taller.

00:00:59.614 --> 00:01:02.420
Now, the zero gravity environment in space

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:04.437
is more extreme than on Mars.

00:01:04.437 --> 00:01:08.736
However, these changes could
still happen on smaller scales.

00:01:08.736 --> 00:01:11.820
And if you compound them
over hundreds of years

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.290
of tens of generations,

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:16.477
the results could be similar
and even more pronounced.

00:01:16.477 --> 00:01:19.790
Basically, humans will
adapt to Mars' conditions

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:21.060
in one of two ways.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:22.960
In the first few centuries,

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.086
our skeletons and muscles
will likely shrink

00:01:25.086 --> 00:01:27.270
and we'll become weaker versions

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.590
of our earthling counterparts.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:32.440
This would almost certainly
lead to shorter lifespans

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:33.890
and health complications.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:36.660
Including neurological
disorders if our skulls shrink

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.240
with the rest of us.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:40.910
So to survive, we may over millennia,

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:43.076
actually undergo the opposite reaction.

00:01:43.076 --> 00:01:45.625
Evolving stronger, more robust bodies

00:01:45.625 --> 00:01:49.820
like the Tharks in Edgar
Rice Burroughs' sci-fi novel,

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:51.213
A Princess of Mars.

00:01:51.213 --> 00:01:55.014
Although, us having six arms
and green skin is less likely.

00:01:55.014 --> 00:01:57.770
In fact, some scientists think

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:01.316
we'd actually develop
orange skin, not green.

00:02:01.316 --> 00:02:03.510
It turns out, the carotenoids
that make carrots,

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:05.730
sweet potatoes, and pumpkins look orange

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:07.470
offer a certain amount of protection

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:10.070
against harmful UV radiation.

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:11.940
When we eat these
pigments in large amounts,

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:14.640
we actually get a boost
of protective carotene

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.910
in our bloodstream and under our skin.

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:20.170
So yeah, eating too many
carrots can turn you orange

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:24.020
but it may also be a great
defense against cancer on Mars.

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:26.840
The planet's thin atmosphere
lets in massive amounts

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:30.428
of UV and other high energy
radiation compared to Earth.

00:02:30.428 --> 00:02:33.300
For example, the average earthling

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.900
receives about three
millisieverts of radiation

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:39.030
per year versus the 30 that
they would get on Mars.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:42.380
To compare, here's the
minimum radiation dose

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:45.896
it takes to drastically
increase your risk of cancer.

00:02:45.896 --> 00:02:50.470
Add up the annual radiation
levels on Mars over a lifetime

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:53.470
and the average martian
would receive 5,000 times

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:55.420
more radiation than someone on Earth.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:58.720
And our big orange
bodies may not just look

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.380
different on the outside.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:03.140
One of the most profound
changes we could have coming

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:05.343
is the next species of human.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.690
Higher levels of
radiation on Mars' surface

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:12.770
would mutate the DNA in our
cells at an accelerated pace.

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:14.910
Normally, a species like homo sapiens

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:18.590
could take a few hundred thousand
years to evolve on Earth.

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:21.240
But some scientists say
the higher mutation rate

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:24.320
could spawn new human
species within centuries.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:26.492
10 times faster than on Earth.

00:03:26.492 --> 00:03:30.320
So, if the radiation
didn't outright kill us,

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:31.910
the mutations that survive

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:34.130
will be passed down through generations.

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:36.300
Ultimately, diversifying the gene pool

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.713
and allowing natural
selection to do its job.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:41.280
As exciting as that may sound,

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.606
there is a potential
downside to all of this.

00:03:43.606 --> 00:03:45.700
If one day in the future,

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:47.640
a martian falls in love with an earthling,

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.311
it can only end in tragedy.

00:03:49.311 --> 00:03:51.850
The immune systems of
martians and earthlings

00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:53.796
will be completely different.

00:03:53.796 --> 00:03:55.240
A meeting between the two

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:57.900
could be just as deadly
as when two foreign groups

00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:59.410
have met in the past.

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:01.760
Like the European
settlers who gave smallpox

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:03.630
to the Native Americans.

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:06.823
Also, depending on how
genetically different they are,

00:04:06.823 --> 00:04:08.628
even if they could meet,

00:04:08.628 --> 00:04:11.910
they may not be able to have
martian earthlings of their own

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:16.050
since only closely related
species can reproduce.

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:17.880
Moving to Mars may be the best chance

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:19.810
for humanity's survival

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:22.913
but it may not be humans
who live there in the end.

